WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.450
Hi there, Steve here in Palm Springs.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.950
Um, I decided to try filming
outside because I had some negative

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:13.260
comments about the redness of
my eyes using the lights inside.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.880
Um, I'm going to talk about spaced
repetition systems, which are very

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.380
popular amongst language learners.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.480
I'm going to give you my perspective
on them and why I don't use them a lot.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.650
Um, down here in Palm Springs,
we've been here for about 12 days.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:29.280
Enjoying it.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.030
It's very warm.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:30.690
It's sunny.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.890
We're having renovations done on
our house in Vancouver, which is

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.120
a good reason to be down here.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.530
Also, we were able to get our
second, uh, vaccine dose, the

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.350
Pfizer, and we appreciate that.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.750
And a minor excitement like a bat flew
in the house a couple of nights ago.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.290
And, uh, we closed it into one
room and the next morning phoned

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.330
the wildlife, uh, who came within
40 minutes and took it away, very

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.980
impressed with the level of service.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.480
Um, now spaced repetition.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.600
Now remember that to me, and I think
this is sort of beyond dispute,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.800
the most important elements in
language learning are your attitude.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.270
In other words, are you enjoying it?

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.660
And second of all, are
you putting in the time?

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.030
Those are the overwhelming issues.

00:01:21.449 --> 00:01:26.190
So, obviously, if you enjoy
doing spaced repetition systems,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.450
then you should do them.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:34.320
Um, even if they are a less efficient
way of spending your time than something

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.980
else, if it increases the amount of
time you spend with the language,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.260
then they are good things to do.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:46.110
Spaced repetition systems, uh,
represented by well-known names,

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:52.050
especially for language learning, like
Anki A N K I and Memrise, and others

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.240
are widely used by language learners.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:01.290
The idea is that there is an
ideal sort of spacing between when

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:06.090
you first encounter a new bit of
information to learn like a word

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:10.680
and when you should next meet that
and then next and next and next again.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:15.450
And so typically in spaced repetition
systems, there are flashcards.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:21.900
And you schedule these new words or
phrases to appear at a certain interval,

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:30.690
which the app will schedule for you
in a way that optimizes your ability

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.579
to retain these bits of information.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.870
Uh, so there's nothing
wrong with doing that.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.990
So why am I not a fan?

00:02:39.079 --> 00:02:43.160
And I should point out too, that at
LingQ, we also use spaced repetition

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.930
systems and we have flashcards.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:52.470
But to me, the use of flashcards,
spaced repetition systems, et cetera,

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:58.740
is the advantage of it is that it's
a randomized way to expose you to

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.240
the language a different way than
let's say, reading or listening.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.930
So it's another way to get at
the same bits of information.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:15.810
Uh, I am not persuaded that the sort of
optimized algorithm achieves a great deal.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:24.510
Um, because you know, it's not like you're
learning objective facts of history, which

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.489
there might be 5,000 that you may need
to learn or 1000 or some such number.

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:34.890
When we're dealing with words, we're
dealing with a situation where the

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:42.540
frequency with which these words appear
in texts declines very, very rapidly, so

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:48.180
that the most frequent 500 words, you're
going to see them so often that you

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.430
almost need and worry about learning them.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.960
But that's when the spaced repetition
system is going to be the most effective.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.700
And I certainly used space repetition
for learning Chinese characters,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:03.780
because I decided that the first
thousand characters, which would

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.070
be very important for me to build
up my vocabulary beyond tha,.

00:04:08.565 --> 00:04:13.035
That I would use this sort of
concentrated way of getting at them

00:04:13.515 --> 00:04:17.115
and each one with all of the different
strokes and stuff like that, I felt

00:04:17.115 --> 00:04:19.065
it would be a useful thing to do.

00:04:19.875 --> 00:04:24.075
Also when I was doing Chinese, I had
six, seven, eight, 10 hours a day.

00:04:24.105 --> 00:04:27.315
However much time I wanted to
spend it was my full-time job.

00:04:27.585 --> 00:04:33.365
So I would still have three hours of
one-on-one lessons with my teacher.

00:04:33.735 --> 00:04:35.445
I would still be reading a lot.

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:36.855
I would be listening a lot.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:42.870
And I would put in an hour or so
a day with these thousand highest

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:48.450
frequency characters using sort of a
self-designed space repetition system.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.730
However, since that time I have
never used space repetition systems.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:05:00.420
Whenever I find myself going through
a long list and remember that in the

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:05.640
languages that I have learned at LingQ,
the number of sort of words that I

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:11.955
have saved, LingQs that I have created
the numbers in the tens of thousands.

00:05:13.305 --> 00:05:13.755
Okay.

00:05:14.115 --> 00:05:19.325
Uh, in a way, the way I create
flashcards at LingQ, every time

00:05:19.325 --> 00:05:22.415
I save a word at LingQ, I have
created a fresh card, flashcard.

00:05:22.415 --> 00:05:27.155
Every time I save a phrase, I've created
a flash card and I do review these when

00:05:27.155 --> 00:05:32.345
I turn the page, when I have just read a
page and there's a bunch of words there

00:05:32.345 --> 00:05:37.125
that either I just saved or I've saved
previously and they've shown up again

00:05:37.845 --> 00:05:39.795
and to kind of refresh them a little bit.

00:05:39.795 --> 00:05:41.445
They hit me again with them.

00:05:41.745 --> 00:05:48.585
I'll review them as I turn the page, but I
will not sit down for a list of, you know,

00:05:48.615 --> 00:05:51.795
5,000, 10,000 words that I have saved.

00:05:51.885 --> 00:05:55.685
In other words, flashcards that
I have remembered or in Anki

00:05:55.705 --> 00:06:00.150
terms, a deck that I have created
simply by reading and listening on

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.530
LingQ, saving words and phrases.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.060
I've got a deck, I've got lots of them.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:10.590
Um, I can even, uh, on LingQ if I tag
them, you know, I can tag them for verbs.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.020
I can tag them for tagging
them for other parts of speech.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:17.550
I can, uh, I can look at only
the ones that, uh, I just met

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:19.200
or the ones that I almost know.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:19.310
And.

00:06:19.724 --> 00:06:23.505
And I've experimented with all of
these different ways of, of going

00:06:23.505 --> 00:06:27.344
through these words in some kind
of a concentrated learning session.

00:06:27.794 --> 00:06:30.135
And I always find that I lose interest.

00:06:30.145 --> 00:06:34.604
So I do it sporadically, but obviously
if you're doing it sporadically,

00:06:34.604 --> 00:06:37.935
you're defeating the purpose of the
spaced repetition system, because

00:06:37.935 --> 00:06:42.135
the spaced repetition system assumes
that there's a certain regularity.

00:06:42.555 --> 00:06:42.974
Okay.

00:06:43.365 --> 00:06:48.255
Now, because of the way in which
frequency, declines so quickly

00:06:49.335 --> 00:06:52.155
the first 500, the first thousand fine.

00:06:52.185 --> 00:06:55.365
You can see them again in
your spaced repetition review.

00:06:55.785 --> 00:06:59.185
You'll probably also come across
them in some form of spaced

00:06:59.205 --> 00:07:03.135
repetition, just through the natural
process of listening and reading.

00:07:03.555 --> 00:07:08.475
But after that, the frequency
declines so quickly that so many of

00:07:08.475 --> 00:07:12.015
the words that you need to learn,
they're only going to show up

00:07:13.034 --> 00:07:16.245
I don't know, twice in a book,
some of them, and yet you

00:07:16.245 --> 00:07:17.145
still don't need to know them.

00:07:17.475 --> 00:07:21.195
So even if you learn them through
some kind of spaced repetition,

00:07:21.225 --> 00:07:22.725
you're likely to forget them again.

00:07:23.835 --> 00:07:29.625
And to try to maintain all 20,000
of them in some kind of a deck that

00:07:29.625 --> 00:07:34.095
you're constantly reviewing would start
to monopolize all your study time.

00:07:35.055 --> 00:07:39.315
So I just, but I repeat what I
said at the beginning, people who

00:07:39.315 --> 00:07:41.145
enjoy doing that should do it.

00:07:41.535 --> 00:07:46.005
I don't ever, when I'm doing flashcards
and I do them from time to time, as I say,

00:07:46.005 --> 00:07:50.940
when I'm turning the page, I never treat
them as a sort of a test of my memory.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.080
I've tried to remember.

00:07:52.409 --> 00:07:54.000
I just treat them as exposures.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.420
In fact, I'll put everything
on the face of the flashcard.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.460
I just flipped through
them as quickly as I can.

00:07:59.909 --> 00:08:05.820
One thing that's good about our flashcards
or activities at LingQ is that we

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.990
have a variety of different activities
that come at you in a randomized way.

00:08:10.365 --> 00:08:13.785
So again, by randomizing
the nature of the activity,

00:08:13.965 --> 00:08:18.105
in other words, it's either a flashcard
or a reverse flashcard or dictation

00:08:18.105 --> 00:08:19.965
or cloze test or multiple choice.

00:08:20.205 --> 00:08:23.505
So that makes it a little more
interesting, but I always come

00:08:23.505 --> 00:08:27.255
away if I ever do sit down for 30
minutes and just to go through a

00:08:27.255 --> 00:08:31.635
deck of say words and phrases, I
really wonder if I achieved anything.

00:08:33.015 --> 00:08:36.015
And of course, if I don't do it
regularly, I won't have achieved anything.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.929
But the idea of spending half an
hour a day when I only have an hour

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:43.530
to spend on language learning to
spend that time on flashcards is not

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:44.939
something that I'm prepared to do.

00:08:45.209 --> 00:08:48.689
The biggest part of my learning
time is simply listening.

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:52.770
There is another issue with flashcards
and I'm not a neuroscientist,

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:57.569
but Manfred Spitzer, whom I often
quote, he is a neuroscientist.

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:00.780
And he says that the brain
has trouble learning details.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:03.689
We forget details there in
our hippocampus, we lose them.

00:09:03.689 --> 00:09:04.260
We forget them.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:10.110
What The brain is best at doing
is creating patterns, recognizing

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:14.130
patterns, creating links, and
that way, becoming familiar with

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:16.260
everything that we encounter in life.

00:09:16.319 --> 00:09:17.310
That's what the brain does.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:23.069
It, it, it enables us to deal with
situations by creating patterns so that we

00:09:23.069 --> 00:09:24.870
start to recognize what's about to happen.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.600
And that's how we become
familiar with a language.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:34.335
And my impression is that if I can
listen and read to content of interest

00:09:34.995 --> 00:09:39.375
without worrying too much about what
I forget and what I remember gradually

00:09:39.375 --> 00:09:43.845
over time, my brain is going to become
familiar with the language and that that

00:09:43.845 --> 00:09:48.585
is a more effective and in my case, more
enjoyable way of learning than trying to

00:09:48.585 --> 00:09:51.465
go through these stacks of flashcards.

00:09:52.245 --> 00:09:58.145
Um, so, uh, and, uh, you know, a big
problem, I think in, in, in Anki for

00:09:58.145 --> 00:10:03.525
many people is even creating these
decks, uh, at least a LingQ, uh, this

00:10:03.525 --> 00:10:07.065
job has done for you because when
you're reading and saving words, you're

00:10:07.065 --> 00:10:12.225
adding, uh, items, words, or phrases to
the deck that you're going to review.

00:10:12.555 --> 00:10:17.055
But I still think that once you're,
uh, you know, the number of words that

00:10:17.055 --> 00:10:21.495
you have saved, the LingQs that you
have created starts to get over 10,000,

00:10:22.215 --> 00:10:27.675
um, I don't know how you can
continue doing justice to the

00:10:27.875 --> 00:10:31.875
principles of these, uh, you know,
the algorithms of spaced repetition.

00:10:32.459 --> 00:10:36.479
Uh, and at the same time, find time
to do the listening and reading and

00:10:36.479 --> 00:10:38.010
speaking that you also need to do.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.430
And, um, so there you have it.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:47.400
Uh, I use, uh, you know, uh, at
LingQ I arbitrarily decide that a

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.530
certain word is no longer status one.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:50.520
It's now status two.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.439
I know it a little better status three.

00:10:52.439 --> 00:10:53.370
I know it even better.

00:10:53.685 --> 00:10:57.824
It's that as far as I know it, then I
move it back again because where is it?

00:10:57.824 --> 00:11:00.525
I thought I really knew all of
a sudden, I don't know them.

00:11:00.795 --> 00:11:02.505
It always bounces back and forth.

00:11:02.895 --> 00:11:07.785
Uh, that's how I study, but I want
to stress again, that the two key

00:11:07.785 --> 00:11:12.495
elements to success in the language
learning are one, keeping a positive

00:11:12.495 --> 00:11:14.055
attitude and enjoying what you're doing.

00:11:14.535 --> 00:11:17.704
And second of all, you know,
finding the time to do it.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:22.350
And so if you are a devotee of
spaced repetition, you enjoy doing

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:26.010
it, that's what you find the time
to do by all means keep doing it.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:27.930
I'm not suggesting people should stop.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:31.380
I'm just explaining why I'm
not a big fan or a big user.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:32.580
There you have it.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.520
And here are a couple of videos
that you might find relevant to

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.880
this whole issue of remembering and
words and how we learn languages.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:40.020
Bye for now.

